PR #1160  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Empty operation() blocks under a data_independent_timing declaration create a spec gap where divergent NaN/subnormal/exception handling leaks data through timing side channels.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-169  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Swaps all S-mode CSRs between domains but never saves/restores fcsr or FP registers f0-f31. The hardware FPU state (accrued exception flags in fcsr.fflags, rounding mode in fcsr.frm, and in-progress register values) is shared across all security domains. Domain A's FP computation residue is directly observable by Domain B, creating a cross-domain covert channel through the FP register file and fcsr. The hart_context struct (lines 24-59) confirms this structurally: sstatus, sie, stvec, sscratch, sepc, scause, stval, sip, satp, scounteren, senvcfg, srmcfg are all saved but fcsr and f0-f31 are absent.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:24-59  hart_context (struct)
      Domain context structure saves all S-mode CSRs but completely lacks fcsr and FP register storage fields. This is the structural root cause of the cross-domain FP state leak. Without storage for FP state, no domain switch can preserve FP isolation. The spec requires data_independent_timing for fdiv.s/fmul.s, but even if OpenSBI emulated these in constant time, the fcsr state (rounding mode, exception flags) would still leak between domains because there is no place to store per-domain FP context.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:113-146  illegal_insn_table
      Table slots 8-11 and 16-27 all point to truly_illegal_insn. FP-computation opcodes (OP-FP=0x53 maps to slot 20, LOAD-FP=0x07 maps to slot 1, MADD-FP opcodes map to slots 16-19) have no constant-time emulation entry. On hardware where fdiv.s/fmul.s have data-dependent execution timing, OpenSBI provides no software fallback to enforce data_independent_timing. The structural gap means the ISA-level assertion of constant-time is never enforced at the firmware layer.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:23-34  truly_illegal_insn
      Constructs CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION trap with the raw fdiv.s/fmul.s instruction encoding as tval and redirects to S-mode via sbi_trap_redirect. No constant-time emulation, no normalization, no sanitization. The hardware's native FPU execution path (with potentially divergent NaN, subnormal, and rounding behavior) is exposed directly to lower privilege levels. When the FPU is disabled and these instructions trap, OpenSBI's response is to pass the raw opcode to S-mode rather than providing a deterministic constant-time emulation.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:148-175  sbi_illegal_insn_handler
      Entry point for all illegal-instruction traps. Dispatches via illegal_insn_table lookup at line 174 using opcode bits [6:2]. FP opcodes fall into truly_illegal_insn slots. This is the single chokepoint where constant-time FP emulation would need to be inserted. Currently, no instruction inspection, no FP opcode decoding, and no mstatus.FS save/restore occurs — if emulation were added here, mstatus.FS would need save/restore to avoid corrupting the lower-mode's FP dirty/clean state.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:32-48  mstatus_init
      Sets MSTATUS_FS=Initial unconditionally when F or D extension is detected via misa_extension check. Enables the FPU for S/U-mode without initializing fcsr rounding mode or exception flags. The hardware reset value of fcsr becomes the effective initial state, creating potential cross-implementation variation in FP behavior. If fcsr comes up with a non-RNE rounding mode and non-zero fflags on some implementations, S-mode software inherits divergent initial FP state.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:191-210  fp_init
      Zeroes all 32 FP registers and clears fcsr (sets frm=RNE, fflags=0) during cold/warm hart init only. Never called on domain context switch. If fdiv.s/fmul.s are used across domains, the fcsr state (rounding mode changes, accrued exception flags from prior FP operations) persists in hardware and leaks between security domains through the shared FPU. The initialization is correct for boot-time but insufficient for runtime domain isolation.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:327-331  sbi_trap_handler
      CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION branch dispatches directly to sbi_illegal_insn_handler without inspecting whether the faulting instruction is an FP op. If constant-time fdiv.s/fmul.s emulation were added to the handler chain, mstatus.FS would need save/restore around the emulation to prevent corrupting the lower-mode's FP dirty/clean state. Currently, the trap handler has no awareness of FP context whatsoever.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:673-690  _trap_handler
      Trap entry saves only integer GPRs via TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 plus mepc and mstatus. FP registers f0-f31 and fcsr are never saved on the exception stack. If OpenSBI's trap handler ever executed FP emulation code for fdiv.s/fmul.s (whether constant-time or not), it would clobber the lower-privilege mode's FP state without restoration. The save/restore infrastructure for FP state simply does not exist at the assembly trap entry/exit.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:103-232  sbi_trap_redirect
      When truly_illegal_insn redirects an FP trap to S-mode, this function writes stval/sepc/scause from the trap info without sanitizing the FP instruction encoding. Lines 202/167 write trap->tval (the raw fdiv.s/fmul.s opcode) directly to STVAL/VSTVAL. The S-mode trap handler receives the unmodified FP instruction encoding and the hardware FPU state (fcsr.fflags, frm, accrued exceptions) is passed through without normalization — S-mode gets no indication that the faulting instruction's timing properties are hardware-dependent.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_emulate_csr.c:44-149  sbi_emulate_csr_read
